//Motion graphics// is a still developing area at the intersection of graphic design, animation, film, 3D rendering, and interactivity.

Basically, graphic designing + one of these other things, resulting in moving designs. This is a pretty standard way motion graphics (aka motion design) is thought of in the design industry.

We’re going to look at a couple other ways of defining and describing “Motion Graphics” — as well as to try and figure out what //motion graphics// is in each of our own opinions …

! Everything is a Motion Graphic!

<<<
A line is a dot that went for a walk
<<< Paul Klee

Things exist, time passes. Posters get hung, people walk past them; every time you raise or touch your phone, something animates; we take a bike ride and read signs and billboards as we travel past them … etc.

Time & motion & change are acting on everything around us and all of our perceptions whether we intend it or not.

!! Motion graphics = everything.

Perhaps that's too easy of an answer? Let's take a little deconstructionist aside …

One might think that the designer decides … I make a motion graphic, and my decision that my design is a motion graphic, that makes it a motion graphic.

A more postmodern take is: I make a graphic and the //reader// (viewer? user?) decides it's a motion graphic and that's what makes it a motion graphic.

I’m meaning here that however the “reader” interprets a design, however it is understood or made meaningful by a viewer and their contexts are right. This is what makes it a motion graphic.

If I think something is a motion graphic based on how I’ve just experienced it, it is one!

!! Sequential Art.

Motion graphics are sometimes grouped into the concept of //sequential art//. Meaning things sequenced in time; this after this after this…

There is an intentional order for how you “read” the piece. Frames are ordered; pages are ordered; the tiles of a composition are ordered; musical notes in a score are ordered; and then the sequence plays out — and is often meant to only be understood — in said order.

Why is a sequence useful? Instead of showing a single picture of a person holding a top hat, we can show two images and now suddenly we have a sequence! — With two points to transition between, we understand that this character is greeting us and not just standing there trying to keep their top hat on.

The same goes with two suns. Yes, a single beautiful picture of the sunset can convey the idea of the sun setting; but! two images in sequence allows us to understand definitively that there's a sunset happening, not just that it's a still picture of a sun … (well, maybe this is a sunrise for a RTL reader).

Simple sequences help us understand things more clearly.

Sequential art covers a lot of ground though: comics, cartoons, ikea instructions, film, newspapers, books, a deck of cards, well organized closets, shelves in color order … perhaps “An intentional sequence in time” is //too// broad a definition for Motion Graphics?

! How is a motion graphic different from just any other sequence?

What “something else” + a sequence = a “motion graphic”?, how do you know you are looking at a //motion designed// sequence as opposed to just any other sequence?

Does a motion graphic have text added? Does a motion graphic have flat, graphic elements?

We’re not talking about cartoon sequences (though maybe motion design is still part of certain aspects of a cartoon!!??);

And, we’re not talking about a feature film (though maybe it's credits or some aspect or scene of the film are motion graphics!?)

We don’t intend to be animators or directors, we want to be designers — a Motion Design sequence //is// different from an animation sequence or a film sequence if it is a designed sequence that designers are making.

!! I know a motion graphic when I see one

There is a certain //something else// that's different about a motion graphic. When you see motion design, you know that it's motion design even though it's probably //also// film or //also// animation or //also// a billboard. You know a motion graphic when you see one!

I tend to think of spotting motion graphics the same way I try to explain liking something. I //know// I like a song or book or sculpture, but I can't always put into words //what// about it makes me like it. I just know “I like this”!

<<<
The first question I ask myself when something doesn't seem to be beautiful is why do I think it's not beautiful. And very shortly you discover that there is no reason.
<<< John Cage

//Noticing// “Motion graphics” shares this; you see the opening titles of a film, & you know that’s motion design, not animation or film editing…

So are all movie titles motion graphics? Are there visual effects that are also motion graphics? Is a purely animated explainer motion graphics? How much of that advertisement I just saw was motion graphics?

! Some things I say are motion graphics...

This is a bit of beating around the bush…

* Primer 2020 / Activating futures opening video, the titles for this conference? https://vimeo.com/showcase/7218620 Stereotype? Of motion graphics?
