!! Now do we know what motion graphics are?

Those examples are all what the industry at large categorizes (more or less) as motion graphics (or motion design). They are biased however, they all integrate a timeline as part of their design. They are made to be seen only as temporal sequences.

Is there a way to capture temporal sequence in a still image? Is that then also a kind of motion graphic? Do you need to USE motion to show motion!?

! You don't have to use motion to show motion …

There are plenty of examples from art and design history of motion being implied or being captured even though the result is a static image.

This photo was taken by Jacques Henri Lartigue in 1913. He was an innovator in strip (or slit) photography, which uses instead of a round aperture, a tiny “strip” aperture that slides across the lens of the camera. You move the slit across the lens along with the thing that's moving; so you capture little slivers of image that add up across the photo. In this example, since Lartigue was panning AND moving the aperture to track the car the still things, like the spectators, end up being warped. You get this cool effect; the driver is moving so fast that he's warped and distorted time and space behind him. It's such a cool image to capture — it captures so much speed in a static image.

(You can do this with the panorama function on most modern cameras/phones now; or with a scanner; or even just as effects in PS and AI… )

… wiggly scan is another way to get the same effects that slit photography got us.

If we also think about motion implying that you are moving around dimensional space over time, the cubists did a pretty extensive job of exploring how you might capture multiple facets of a still life but still present them all in a static plane. So I would say this is a motion piece also just because it implies moving in and around a space.

The Scrabble Game, by David Hockney, 1982. See also https://frankchimero.com/blog/2015/the-webs-grain/

Sort of like the cubist paintings, these photo explorations by David Hockney combine multiple moments and vantage points into single images; motion in a static frame.

If you're into comics, that’s a medium that's really tried to explore in so many different ways how a simple, still pane might convey a lot of motion to a reader. Showing motion in this context typically involves putting some lines behind things. You add these motion lines, and then maybe people will understand that those things are moving.

Or if you think about musical scores (particularly “graphic scores”), this is an avant-garde alternative way of scoring music. This is obviously not a traditional musical score. But that's an attempt to visually represent something that can only happen like out in time. But you're representing it as a static printed thing.

!! Now for something that ties all this together.

Can we now define and describe “Motion Graphics” more clearly than “you’ll know it when you see it” or “an intentional sequence?” How do we define motion graphics so that what the industry and large means is covered; that web and app and interface design fit into the definition; watching an airplane fly overhead or driving down the highway fits; and that time and space explorations more broadly, even those resulting in static mediums fit into the definition? What is our unifying theory of motion design?

! Finally, a definition?

There are several definitions for Motion Graphics out there:

The most common is for sure some variant of: //Animated Graphic Design//

Or maybe, //Communicating using animation and/or video…// and its this communication angle that sets Motion Graphics apart from other animation/film based mediums.

Ian Robinson, who does a lot of MoGraph tutorials, defines motion graphics as: //Type, colors, etc. — the building blocks of design — in motion!//

Biteable.com says: //Motion graphics is animation, but with text as a major component. Essentially, it’s animated graphic design.//

But, none of those definitions help us understand how Motion Graphics is different than Animation other than just our gut reaction.

Wikipedia says:

<<<
Motion graphic design is a subset of graphic design in that it uses graphic design principles in a filmmaking or video production context (or other temporally evolving visual media) through the use of animation or filmic techniques. Examples include the kinetic typography and graphics used in film and television opening sequences, and the spinning, three-dimensional station identification logos of some television channels. The art form has been around for decades, and has advanced in technical sophistication over time.
<<<

Okay so that defines most of the video stuff I showed you, and starts to hint at other formats with the “temporally evolving visual media...

99Designs.com explains it as:

<<<
Motion graphics takes graphic design that would be otherwise static and gives it animation and movement, usually without following a specific narrative. / Motion graphics focus on giving movement to graphic design elements, but tend to have less of a concrete storytelling aspect than other types of animation.
<<<

Okay, that’s something a little less “you’ll know it when you see it” …

It’s important to think that Motion Graphics IS NOT character animation; and motion design IS NOT visual effects; and motion design IS NOT film editing or directing … But it might be added to or a part of those things. Where’s the boundary? Is it where you cross over into characters expressing themselves; or serious, long form narratives/storytelling?

Here’s my definition for what a motion graphic is that also doesn’t force a medium or technology upon it. We’ll break Motion Graphics down:

# Motion
# Graphics (graphic design)

''So motion.'' Motion = Change over time. Any change.

''Graphics…'' that’s meant to be short for graphic design.

[[Paul Rand]] had what I think is a good definition for graphic design: //Design is the relationship between form and content.// But I do think that’s a little out of date, so I’m going to add to that a bit; //design is the relationships between form and content and context(s).//

Combing that all together gets us:

!! Motion Graphics = A change in relationships between form and content and context.

To me, this describes all manner of things that might “change” purposely or accidentally in a design. This is triggered if a designer intentionally moves things around. This is triggered if a “reader” or “viewer” changes their relationship to a work. It handles cars driving by a billboard. It handles film titles. It handles different readers in different times and spaces; And! We get away from just being any sequence in time; or based on design + animation or design + film...

I like this as well because defining motion graphics this way really emphasizes the “relationships” part. And Motion is really all about relationships anyway; or at least understanding that motion is supposed to be happening in a visual.

"""
One thing moves, others don’t.
One thing moves, others follow suit.
One thing moves, others resist
…
"""

So, I think this defines the status quo kind of motion designing better AND it fits my broader EVERYTHING IS A MOTION GRAPHIC view too.

! This is the End

So, what is a motion graphic? We should be able to answer that question now — even if the answer is just “I know it when I see it!”

We can think of everything as a motion graphic; because no matter what, relationships are always changing between form, content, and contexts of designed objects and their “readers” …

! The 2022 proposal

Everything is a Motion Graphic: Expanding the definition of graphic design in motion. A proposal for a long paper at the MODE Summit, written with Hayelin Choi in October 2022, starting from the lecture above.

!! Abstract

Looking up various definitions of “Motion Graphics” one learns that the discipline is seen as basically “animated graphic design.” But what does this mean? As screens have permeated every surface of our lives, it is easy to think of “motion graphics” or “animated graphic design” increasingly in terms of just that — graphic design moving on screens, designed animations, or cleverly crafted additions to existing video content.

However, time, motion, and change are acting on everything around us, and all of our perceptions, whether we intend it to or not. Things exist, time passes. Posters get hung, people walk past them. We take a bike ride and read signs and billboards as we travel alongside. The livery of an airplane brand zooms above us. When we visit a supermarket or a mall with music in the background, we amble around dimensional and typographic sculpture. As we are experiencing the change, our eyes function like a film camera. Our eyes pan, focus, and pause to read a headline, a stop sign, and a bag of chips. We see an advertisement from our childhood years later and still feel moved. Motion graphics are happening around us all the time, but our traditional definition of motion graphics is inadequate to explore this new perspective within the realms of education.

These other design scenarios are further chances to intentionally think about “motion graphics” and how students encounter it in academia. Through a variety of examples we will present motion design definitions that allow concepts of change over time to encompass not only animated designs on screens, but also design as it exists out in the real world, traveling and changing through time.

The end goal is to see //everything// as a motion graphic, which can lead to the start of a different pedagogical approach to motion graphics. This challenges educators to focus more on //time + motion + change// in graphic design — not necessarily on software and technology. To facilitate a vision of motion graphics as foundational in design curriculum, we must redesign motion graphics education to be more accessible for both students and design faculty from the very beginning.

!! Broad outline

* Provoke with the initial starting question: How do you know something is a motion graphic?
* Provide quick, basic working definitions of motion graphics from the field,
* Explain through historical and contemporary examples how this definition is limited
* Provide new definition that encompasses other ways “motion” or “change” might still live in non-traditional motion design works
* Deliver examples that fit into new definition
* Lay out how //time + motion + change// can become more foundational design curriculum concepts.
* Persuade the audience that everything is a motion graphic.

!! Keywords

Perception, continuity, sequence, movement, Change

